        <p>Two schedules were lost and only two matches with them, because every other match was listed by a school whose schedule arrived.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Red-pen the report</h3>
    <p>The 2025 season file holds {unseen.held} conference matches, and box scores for {unseen.withBox} of them. Kick has written five sentences about the season. Keep the ones the record and those counts support. Strike the ones they do not.</p>
    <div class="cv-check" data-once="report">
      {report.map((r) => (
        <fieldset class="cv-line" data-q data-type="radio" data-answer={r.answer} data-why={r.why}>
          <legend>“{r.text}”</legend>
          <label><input type="radio" name={`rep-${r.key}`} value="keep" data-save={`rep-${r.key}`} /><span>Keep</span></label>
          <label><input type="radio" name={`rep-${r.key}`} value="strike" data-save={`rep-${r.key}`} /><span>Strike</span></label>
          <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
        </fieldset>
      ))}
      <p class="cv-once">One attempt.</p>
      <button type="button" data-submit>Check my marks</button>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status" data-need></p>
      <div class="cv-reveal">
        <p>Three counts describe this one season, and all three are true: {unseen.schedules} of 7 schedules, {unseen.held} of {unseen.expected} matches, and box scores for {unseen.withBox} of {unseen.expected}. Each counts a different unit.</p>
        <p>Ten scheduled matches have no box score in the file: the two that were lost, plus eight more. All eight are matches the season file took from Harbor Point's schedule, and Harbor Point no longer serves box scores. {harborDark} of the eight were against Dunmore or Eastbrook State, so no collected page could have supplied them. The other {8 - harborDark} were against schools whose own sites were collected. This sample takes a box score only from the site its fixture was kept from, so those are gaps a second source could close. The real platform tries the box-score link from either school, so it closes a gap like these whenever the other school published a working link.</p>
        <p>The third sentence deserves a second look, because it is close to what Touchline itself prints. The platform's match figures count “played” from the matches it holds, so a figure built that way for this season would read {unseen.withBox} of {unseen.held}. That is a true statement about the file. It becomes false only when someone reads it as a statement about the season. What stops that reading is the pair of named gaps beside it: two schedules not collected. A denominator built from collected pages always needs its gaps printed next to it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <label class="cv-write" for="cv-caption">Now write the line that goes under the 2025 conference table when it is published.</label>
    <div class="cv-commit" data-commit>
      <textarea id="cv-caption" rows="3" data-save="caption"></textarea>
      <button type="button" disabled>Compare with a worked answer</button>
      <p class="cv-once" data-needs-note>Write a full sentence to compare.</p>
      <div class="cv-reveal">
        <p class="cv-model">Built from {unseen.held} of {unseen.expected} conference matches. Not collected: both meetings of Dunmore and Eastbrook State. Each of those two schools has two more scheduled matches than this table shows.</p>
        <fieldset class="cv-ticks">
          <legend>Read your line again and mark it honestly.</legend>
          <label><input type="checkbox" data-save="cap-count" /><span>It gives the count and the total</span></label>
          <label><input type="checkbox" data-save="cap-gap" /><span>It names the gap, so a reader can tell which rows to distrust</span></label>
          <label><input type="checkbox" data-save="cap-true" /><span>It states nothing about two results nobody has seen</span></label>
        </fieldset>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="transfer">
    <h2>6. Transfer: the same number, twice</h2>
    <p>A health network has ten clinics that report patient visits each week. In week 9 you hold this, in two different worlds.</p>
    <div class="cv-twins">
      <div>
        <h3>World A</h3>
        <p>Eight clinics reported visits, 100 in total.</p>
        <p>The other two each sent a file with a header and no rows. Both were closed that week for refitting, and the closure notices are on file.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>World B</h3>
        <p>Eight clinics reported visits, 100 in total.</p>
        <p>The other two sent nothing. Nobody has heard from them.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>The spreadsheet is identical in both: eight rows, sum 100. Write the headline you would publish for each.</p>
    <div class="cv-commit" data-commit>
      <label class="cv-write" for="cv-a">World A headline</label>
      <textarea id="cv-a" rows="2" data-save="transfer-a"></textarea>
      <label class="cv-write" for="cv-b">World B headline</label>
      <textarea id="cv-b" rows="2" data-save="transfer-b"></textarea>
      <button type="button" disabled>Compare with worked answers</button>
      <p class="cv-once" data-needs-note>Write a full sentence in each box to compare.</p>
      <div class="cv-reveal">
        <p class="cv-model">A: 100 visits across all ten clinics. Two were closed.</p>
        <p class="cv-model">B: At least 100 visits, from eight of ten clinics. Two have not reported.</p>
        <p>World A's two clinics are <em>empty</em>: they answered, the answer is proved, and it is zero. World B's are <em>unavailable</em>. The number 100 is a total in A and a lower bound in B.</p>
        <fieldset class="cv-ticks">
          <legend>Read your World B headline again and mark it honestly.</legend>
          <label><input type="checkbox" data-save="self-floor" /><span>It reads as a lower bound, not a total</span></label>
          <label><input type="checkbox" data-save="self-count" /><span>It says eight of ten</span></label>
          <label><input type="checkbox" data-save="self-true" /><span>It would still be true if a late file arrived</span></label>
        </fieldset>
      </div>
    </div>
    <h3>World B, on Thursday</h3>
    <p>One of World B's silent clinics sends a late file: 300 visits, after it took in patients from a hospital that flooded. “At least 100” stayed true. “100 visits” would have been low by at least a factor of four, and the flood that filled the clinic is also what delayed its report. The size of the number and its lateness had the same cause. Unless that cause is itself recorded, so that you can condition on it, this is the case statisticians call missing not at random.</p>
    <div class="cv-check" data-once="clinic">
      <div data-q data-type="range" data-answer="400,520" data-why="400 to 520, from nine of ten clinics. The floor is what you hold. The ceiling adds the most the silent clinic could have seen: 5 days at 24. A documented limit on the missing unit turns a lower bound into an interval, as the points rule did for Dunmore.">
        <p class="cv-qlabel">You now hold 400 visits from nine clinics. The tenth is still silent. It is the network's smallest clinic: its licence allows at most 24 visits a day, and it opens five days a week. What are the lowest and highest week 9 totals the network could have?</p>
        <div class="cv-row"><label>Lowest <input inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" data-save="clinic-low" /></label><label>Highest <input inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" data-save="clinic-high" /></label></div>
        <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
      </div>
      <h3>World C</h3>
      <p>Eight clinics reported, 100 visits in total. The other two each sent a file with a header and no rows. Nothing in either file says which week it covers, and there is no closure notice.</p>
      <div class="cv-line" data-q data-type="select" data-answer="unavailable" data-why="Unavailable. A file arrived, and it cannot prove it is week 9's file. The same rule as a schedule page that cannot prove its season. Having no reader would mean the file could not be opened at all.">
        <label><span>What state are those two clinics in for week 9?</span>
          <select data-save="clinic-state"><option value="">Choose</option>{stateOptions.map(([k, n]) => <option value={k}>{n}</option>)}</select>
        </label>
        <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
      </div>
      <div class="cv-line" data-q data-type="select" data-answer="floor" data-why="A lower bound, from eight of ten. Two zero-row files that cannot prove their week tell you nothing about week 9.">
        <label><span>What is the number 100?</span>
          <select data-save="clinic-claim"><option value="">Choose</option><option value="total">The network's total for week 9</option><option value="floor">A lower bound for week 9</option><option value="ceiling">An upper bound for week 9</option><option value="eight">The total for week 9, since the two files show zero</option><option value="nothing">Not usable at all</option></select>
        </label>
        <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
      </div>
      <p class="cv-once">One attempt.</p>
      <button type="button" data-submit>Check all three</button>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status" data-need></p>
      <div class="cv-reveal"><p>World C holds the same files as World A and supports only World B's headline, because the files cannot prove their week.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="brief">
    <h2>7. Your brief</h2>
    <p>Riley handed over a job without knowing what to require. So finish by writing the requirement. Your part 4 prompt was written for one job. Turn it into a standing clause: what you would tell any AI before it collects anything for you, in any field. Add one acceptance example, a case with a known right answer that would catch a bad implementation.</p>
    <div class="cv-mark"><div>
      <p class="cv-who">The prompt you rewrote in part 4</p>
      <p class="cv-yousaid"><span data-show="rewrite">nothing yet</span></p>
    </div></div>
    <div class="cv-commit" data-commit>
      <label class="cv-write" for="cv-brief">Your coverage clause, and one acceptance example</label>
      <textarea id="cv-brief" rows="6" data-save="brief"></textarea>
      <button type="button" disabled>Compare with a worked clause</button>
      <p class="cv-once" data-needs-note>Write a full sentence to compare.</p>
      <div class="cv-reveal">
        <p class="cv-model cv-pre">{model}</p>
        <fieldset class="cv-ticks">
          <legend>Yours does not need these words. It needs five things. Mark the ones it has.</legend>
          <label><input type="checkbox" data-save="brief-set" /><span>An expected set, with where it comes from</span></label>
          <label><input type="checkbox" data-save="brief-state" /><span>A named state for every expected item</span></label>
          <label><input type="checkbox" data-save="brief-success" /><span>Success defined as a true record</span></label>
          <label><input type="checkbox" data-save="brief-denominator" /><span>A denominator on every published figure</span></label>
          <label><input type="checkbox" data-save="brief-test" /><span>An acceptance example with a known right answer</span></label>
        </fieldset>
        <p>Anything unmarked is worth a second pass. Edit your clause above until all five hold.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cv-row"><button type="button" data-copy-brief>Copy my clause</button><span class="cv-saved" role="status" data-saved-note></span></div>
    <div class="cv-say">
      <p class="cv-who">Riley, to the eight schools <span>Monday, 8:10</span></p>
      <p>Corrected table attached. It counts {story.afterRepair} of {story.finals} finished conference matches. Dunmore's page did not answer on Friday, and its seven home results are now taken from the visitors' pages. Hartwell Tech is second. Cedar Grove against St. Lambert on September 21 is final with no score on either schedule. Its box score page has not been checked yet, so both schools are one match short.</p>
    </div>
    <p data-close-first hidden>Your question from the film was: <q data-close-first-text></q> Would the coverage record have answered it?</p>

    <details class="cv-more">
      <summary>Going deeper: collecting again without starting over</summary>
      <p>A daily run across a thousand sites cannot download everything every day. The collector keeps a short tag the site hands out with each page. On the next run it sends the tag back and asks whether the page has changed. The site answers “unchanged” or sends the new page. In the story, the next morning Dunmore answers again and St. Lambert has changed one entry, so the collector downloads two pages, {bytes(run.rerunBytes)} bytes, where a full run moves {bytes(run.allBytes)}.</p>
      <p>The tag is an <strong>ETag</strong>, and the question is a <strong>conditional request</strong>: send the page only if it has changed. An “unchanged” answer confirms the square in the state it already had, without a new download.</p>
      <p>A tag must never outlive the page it describes. If the collector held a tag for a page it had failed to save, the site would keep answering “unchanged” about a copy that does not exist, and nothing would ever flag it. So a tag whose page is not on disk is treated as no tag at all. Some sites send no ETag, so the collector also keeps a hash of each page's content. A hash cannot save the download. It saves reading an unchanged page a second time.</p>
      <p>The repository has a small lab for this, with no network: <code>python labs/m01_collect.py</code> runs three days of collection against a pretend web and prints what was downloaded each day. A good exercise for your assistant: ask it to make the pretend web lie, by sending the same tag for a changed page, then explain what breaks and how a collector could notice.</p>
    </details>
    <button type="button" class="cv-quiet" data-reset>Clear my answers and start this module again</button>
  </section>
</div>

